# Stipulation defense

Applies when the work is actually a stipulation, extension, stay,
continuance, or proposed order that would move a defense clock, waive a
right, expand their relief, or put his signature on counsel's paper.

Does **not** apply to ordinary Plaintiff papers (status reports,
oppositions, complaints, hearing notes, discovery plans) or to answering
a court question. Do not recite "this is not a stipulation" or "counsel
may not affix" on those papers.

Use discretion. Be firm on extra time, stays, waivers, floating resets,
and a conformed `/s/`. Treat authenticity, a name correction he already
wants, advancing a hearing onto his calendar, and a yes to the assigned
magistrate as settlement judge as ordinary.

When it applies:

- Copy `.claude/skills/stipulation-defense/TEMPLATE.md`. Fill the brackets.
- Match the terms in `.claude/skills/stipulation-defense/SKILL.md`.
- Use drop-in language from `.claude/skills/stipulation-defense/CLAUSES.md`.
- Draft **most favorably to Plaintiff**. Every extra day, extra party, extra
  waiver, extra silence, or extra item they did not ask for is a gift.
- **Defense clocks:** shorten if the change helps Plaintiff, or hold the
  existing date. Never volunteer extra days. Never imply a second extension.
- **Plaintiff clocks:** do not compress his opposition, disclosure, demand,
  or default right. Continue the event only — not the chain of deadlines.
- **Date certain.** Never "reset to run from the continued conference."
- Do **not** sign counsel's paper. Return a Plaintiff counterpart.
- Plaintiff signs his own paper. Counsel may not affix `/s/` or e-signature.
- Named party only. Named deadline only. Court-set dates stay unless moving
  them helps Plaintiff. Preserve default and every other right.

Portfolio-wide. Matter 29 (Amazon / Accurate counterparts) and Matter 2
(Li stay counter-draft) are the positive examples. Serrato Dkt. 22 is the
failure mode — do not expand their ask. Not their only cases.
